  "contents": "Does the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 (Ontario) make this clause of a residential tenancy agreement void?\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nDecide using only the sections of the Act below. They are a selection, not the whole Act.\n\n<sections>\n[The headings and text of sections 4, 14, 15, 20, 22, 25, 26, 27, 34, 37, 40, 105, 106, 108, 116, 119, 120, 134 of the Act, from e-Laws; SHA-256 170f924284b858615f0e872622e0e1150d171bcaeffe1a8e61a2ea40a42b0e8c. Left out of saved records.]\n</sections>\n\n<clause>\nThe Landlord may come into the unit without written notice whenever the Tenant, at that time, agrees to let the Landlord in.\n</clause>\n\nAnswer with:\n- verdict: \"void\" if the Act makes the clause void, \"allowed\" if the Act permits it, or \"unsure\" if these sections don't let you decide.\n- section: the number of the one section that decides it, such as \"14\", without a subsection. Empty if unsure.\n- quote: the words of that section that decide it, copied exactly: one continuous passage from a single subsection, at least five words, with nothing left out, added or changed. Not a heading. Empty if unsure.\n- reason: one or two sentences.\n",
